    {
      "question": "e-8216",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "80dc0cba4b127e7fc71c194d9c5f4fe9222e463f869aa36ee8a09fa070dca6c4",
      "new_sha256": "e46f74a90e041d35f09db85b4c1fca216b9b8b8c1aa299fa0407cbb48ee6cc20",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-8216",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "To find the $z$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{4}+\\frac{4}{2} & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ 1+2 & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ 9 & =(z+1)^{2} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-4,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,2).$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042648/example-8216.json",
          "file_sha256": "3049fd772a0b384d73922cf659f20e46a83a51b012ca456c5221eb0397366a1c"
